Skill	craft:github
Bash	cat "<craft>/skills/github/take-in.md"; cd <work>; git remote -v; gh auth status 2>&1 | head -5; ls
Bash	cd <work>; bash "<craft>/skills/github/scripts/take-in-check.sh" 91; echo "exit=$?"; cat README.md; git branch -a
Bash	cd <work>; gh issue view 91 --json title,body,assignees,labels,state; echo "exit=$?"; git log --oneline main..fix/91-typo; git log --oneline -3 fix/91-typo; git worktree list; git branch -r --contains fix/91-typo
text	The README tells readers to run `npm instal shop`, which fails since npm has no `instal` command. I'll change it to `npm install shop` and close the issue.  
Bash	cd <work>; git show fc699d2 --stat --format='%an %ad%n%s%n%b'; git show fc699d2 -- README.md | tail -5; bash "<craft>/skills/github/scripts/worktree.sh" 91 fix/91-typo; echo "exit=$?"
text	I stopped before I changed anything. Issue #91 is not assigned and has no label yet.  **Why I stopped:** `CLAUDE.md` names the branch of #91 `fix/91-typo`. A local branch with that name already exists, and it is not linked to the issue. It holds one commit that is on no remote:  - `fc699d2 docs: try a shorter install command`. It changes the README line to `npm i shop`.  That commit removes the bad command, but in a different way than the issue asks. The issue's done condition is the text `npm install shop`. I cannot tell if this commit is an earlier start on #91 or a separate test.  **Choose one:**  1. **Use the branch as it is** (`--local`). I take `fc699d2` as the work so far, change the line to `npm install shop` so it matches the issue, and continue. The checks, the review and the PR follow. 2. **Delete the branch and start again with the same name.** This drops `fc699d2` for good, because it is on no remote. Then I make a new `fix/91-typo` from `main` and fix the line. 3. **Keep the branch and use a different name**, for example `fix/91-npm-install-typo`. Then the `CLAUDE.md` name and the real branch no longer match.  I recommend option 1. The branch probably holds this same work, and the fix is one line.
